var FIELDS=["overview", "operations", "implementation", "applications", "complexity", "interview", "variants", "real-world", "practice"];
var ARCHIVE=[{"id": "JAH-STACKS-000001", "title": "Stack Overview: What It Is and Why It Matters", "d": "The stack: last in, first out — a pile of plates with strict manners.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nA stack is an ordered collection with one rule: the last item added is the first removed — last-in, first-out, or LIFO. Think of a spring-loaded plate dispenser in a cafeteria: you can only take the top plate, and new plates go on top. The operations are push (add to the top), pop (remove from the top), and peek (look at the top without removing it).\n\nStacks matter because nesting is everywhere in computing. Every function call pushes a frame onto the call stack and pops it on return — the entire execution of your program is stack discipline. Undo systems push states and pop to rewind; browsers push pages and pop on Back; parsers push opening brackets and pop on closing ones; depth-first search is a stack exploring as deep as possible before backtracking.\n\nLike the queue, the stack deliberately hides everything but one end — but where the queue is about fairness, the stack is about recency and nesting. That restriction buys O(1) everything and beautifully simple reasoning: the stack's state is exactly the history of unclosed beginnings. Learn the stack and you understand function calls, backtracking, and undo in one stroke.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000002", "title": "Stack: How It Works — Core Operations", "d": "Push, pop, peek, and the top-pointer machinery that makes them O(1).", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nA stack needs only one end, so the implementation is almost embarrassingly simple: an array plus a top index (or a linked list with a head pointer). Push writes at top and increments; pop decrements and returns; peek reads without moving. No shifting, no searching, no wraparound needed for the basic version — every operation touches exactly one element: O(1) time, O(1) space.\n\nThe dynamic version grows like a dynamic array (doubling when full, amortized O(1) push). The linked version pushes/pops nodes at the head in O(1) with no capacity limit. Underflow — popping an empty stack — is the error case; safe implementations raise rather than return garbage. That's nearly the whole story: the stack's simplicity is its power, and its power shows up in what it enables — recursion, parsing, backtracking — rather than in its own machinery.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "operations": [{"op": "push", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "write at top, increment pointer"}, {"op": "pop", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "decrement pointer, return element"}, {"op": "peek / top", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "read top without moving"}, {"op": "isEmpty / size", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "top == base; maintained counter"}], "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000003", "title": "Stack: Internals, Trade-offs and When to Use It", "d": "Array vs linked stacks, the call-stack contract, and where LIFO is the honest model.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nInternally a stack is an array with a top index or a singly-linked list used head-first. The array version is cache-friendly and has zero per-element overhead; the linked version never needs resizing and never wastes capacity. Both give O(1) push/pop — pick by memory behavior: bounded known depth favors arrays, unbounded or huge elements favor linked lists (or just use the language's dynamic array as the backing).\n\nThe call stack deserves special attention because it's a stack with a contract: each frame holds the return address, parameters, and locals, and the LIFO discipline is what makes recursion work — each call's state is preserved beneath the next. Stack overflow is the failure mode: too-deep recursion (or an infinite one) exhausts the fixed call-stack memory, typically 1–8 MB. That's why deep recursion gets rewritten as iteration with an explicit stack.\n\nReach for a stack when the problem nests: matching brackets, undo, backtracking, DFS, expression evaluation, function-call modeling. Reach past it when you need fairness (queue), both ends (deque), or key access (hash table). The stack's question is always 'what was the most recent unclosed thing?' — when that's your question, nothing beats it.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000004", "title": "Stack Push", "d": "Adding to the top: O(1), the stack's signature move.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nPush adds an element at the top: write to slots[top], increment top. One write, one increment — O(1) time, O(1) space, no element moves, no searching. In a dynamic stack, a full buffer triggers the doubling resize (O(n) copy, amortized O(1) per push — the same protocol as dynamic arrays). In a linked stack, push allocates one node and swings the head pointer.\n\nPush is where the stack's history accumulates: every push is a promise that a future pop will undo it, and the stack remembers the exact reverse order automatically. Function calls push frames, parsers push open brackets, editors push states — all relying on push being cheap enough to do millions of times without thought.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "push", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "write at top, increment; doubling resize when full (amortized O(1))"}], "program": "st = []\nst.append('a')   # push — O(1)\nst.append('b')\nst.append('c')\nprint(st)        # ['a', 'b', 'c'] — top is the right end", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1); amortized O(1) under doubling growth. The top pointer is always known — nothing else moves.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000005", "title": "Stack Pop", "d": "Removing from the top: O(1), undoing the most recent push.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nPop removes and returns the top element: decrement top, return slots[top]. Like push it touches one element: O(1) time, O(1) space. The LIFO guarantee is structural — the top pointer only ever retreats through the exact reverse of the push order, so the most recent un-popped push is always served next.\n\nPopping an empty stack (underflow) is the error case: raise, don't return a sentinel that callers might mistake for data. Some dynamic stacks shrink when mostly empty (quarter-full hysteresis, as with arrays), but many don't bother — a stack that grew large and shrank usually grows again. Note the asymmetry with queues: pop needs no wraparound arithmetic, just a retreating pointer — the stack is the simpler machine.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "pop", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "decrement top, return element; underflow on empty must raise"}], "program": "st = ['a', 'b', 'c']\nprint(st.pop())  # c — most recent, served first\nprint(st.pop())  # b\nprint(st)        # ['a']", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1): one pointer retreat, one read. Underflow must raise — never return a sentinel as data.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000006", "title": "Stack Peek and Top", "d": "Seeing the top without popping: O(1) and non-destructive.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nPeek (top) returns the top element without moving the pointer — a read-only look at the most recent item. O(1), changes nothing. It's the safe way to inspect before deciding: a parser peeks to see if the top is the matching open bracket, a min-stack peeks to report the current minimum, DFS peeks to check the current path's head.\n\nPeeking an empty stack is underflow, same as pop — guard with isEmpty. The peek/pop separation mirrors the queue's peek/dequeue split: look without consuming, consume only when committed. In expression evaluation and bracket matching, the algorithm is often 'peek to decide, pop to commit,' and getting that rhythm right is half of stack programming.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "peek / top", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "read slots[top-1] without moving the pointer"}], "program": "st = ['a', 'b', 'c']\nprint(st[-1])   # c — peek, nothing removed\nprint(len(st))  # 3 — size unchanged", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1), non-destructive. Peek-to-decide, pop-to-commit is the core stack-programming rhythm.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000007", "title": "Stack isEmpty and Size", "d": "O(1) bookkeeping: the top pointer IS the size.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nisEmpty checks top == 0 (or head is None in the linked version); size is top itself — the pointer doubles as the counter, so there's nothing extra to maintain. Both O(1), both trivially correct, which is part of why stacks are so reliable: there's almost no bookkeeping to get wrong.\n\nGuard every pop and peek with isEmpty. In parsers, an unexpected empty stack means unbalanced input (a closing bracket with no opener) — it's a diagnosis, not just an error. After processing, a non-empty stack means unclosed openers — the second half of the balance check. The stack's emptiness is meaningful state; treat it as a signal, not just a guard.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "isEmpty", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "top == 0"}, {"op": "size", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "the top pointer itself"}], "program": "st = []\nprint(len(st) == 0)  # True — isEmpty\nst.append('x')\nprint(len(st))       # 1 — the pointer is the count", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1): the top pointer doubles as the size counter. Emptiness after processing is a diagnostic signal.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000008", "title": "The Call Stack", "d": "How function calls really work: frames pushed on entry, popped on return.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nEvery function call pushes a frame onto the call stack: the return address, the arguments, and space for locals. When the function returns, its frame pops and control resumes at the return address with the caller's frame on top again. Nested calls nest frames — main calls f calls g gives a stack [main, f, g] — and recursion is just a function pushing frames of itself. This is why local variables 'remember' their values across a recursive call: each frame has its own copy.\n\nThe call stack is fixed-size (typically 1–8 MB), so deep recursion overflows it — Python's default recursion limit is 1000 frames, a guard against this. Each frame costs tens to hundreds of bytes; a million-deep recursion needs an explicit heap stack instead. Tail-call optimization (which Python deliberately lacks) would reuse the frame, but the portable answer is iteration + explicit stack. Understanding the call stack turns 'recursion' from magic into machinery: it's pushes and pops, all the way down.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "applications", "operations": [{"op": "call", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "push frame: return address + args + locals"}, {"op": "return", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "pop frame, resume at return address"}], "program": "import sys\nprint(sys.getrecursionlimit())  # 1000 — the guard against overflow\n\ndef fact(n, depth=0):\n    print('  ' * depth + f'fact({n}) pushed')\n    r = 1 if n \u003c= 1 else n * fact(n - 1, depth + 1)\n    print('  ' * depth + f'fact({n}) popped -> {r}')\n    return r\n\nfact(3)", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Call/return are O(1) stack ops. Depth is bounded by fixed stack memory — deep recursion needs an explicit heap stack.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000009", "title": "Expression Evaluation with Stacks", "d": "Infix to postfix and back: the stack as a calculator.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nArithmetic nesting is stack-shaped: in 3 + 4 × 2, the × binds tighter, and the classic algorithm (Dijkstra's shunting-yard) uses an operator stack to handle precedence — push operators, pop and apply when a higher-or-equal precedence operator arrives, parentheses push/pop as grouping. The output, postfix (Reverse Polish) notation, then evaluates with a value stack: push numbers, on an operator pop two operands, apply, push the result.\n\nPostfix evaluation is the cleanest stack algorithm there is: 3 4 2 × + evaluates as push 3, push 4, push 2, × pops 4,2 pushes 8, + pops 3,8 pushes 11 — O(n) time, O(n) space, no precedence logic at eval time. This is how calculators, Forth, PostScript, and JVM bytecode work: the machine is a stack, and expressions are programs for it. Bracket matching is the same idea with symbols instead of numbers.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "applications", "operations": [{"op": "postfix eval", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "value stack: push numbers, pop-apply-push on operators"}, {"op": "infix -> postfix", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "operator stack handles precedence (shunting-yard)"}], "program": "def eval_rpn(tokens):\n    st = []\n    for t in tokens:\n        if t in '+-*/':\n            b, a = st.pop(), st.pop()\n            st.append({'+': a+b, '-': a-b, '*': a*b, '/': int(a/b)}[t])\n        else:\n            st.append(int(t))\n    return st[0]\n\nprint(eval_rpn(['3', '4', '2', '*', '+']))  # 11  (3 + 4*2)\nprint(eval_rpn(['2', '1', '+', '3', '*']))  # 9   ((2+1)*3)", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Postfix eval O(n) with a value stack; shunting-yard O(n) with an operator stack. The stack IS the calculator.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000010", "title": "Monotonic Stacks", "d": "The stack that stays sorted: next-greater-element in O(n).", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nA monotonic stack maintains its elements in increasing (or decreasing) order by popping violators on each push: to push x onto an increasing stack, pop while top > x, then push. The result: the stack is always sorted, and each element is pushed once and popped once — O(n) total for n pushes, amortized O(1) each.\n\nThis powers the 'next greater element' family: for each array element, the next element to its right that's larger. Scan left to right with a decreasing stack of indices; when a new element exceeds the stack top, it is the next-greater for everything it pops. Daily temperatures, largest rectangle in histogram, stock spans, trapping rain water — all monotonic-stack problems. The pattern: the stack remembers candidates, and each new element retires the ones it dominates. Brute force is O(n²); the monotonic stack is O(n).\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "applications", "operations": [{"op": "monotonic push", "complexity": "O(1) amortized", "note": "pop violators, then push; each element popped at most once"}, {"op": "next greater element", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "one left-to-right pass with a decreasing stack"}], "program": "def next_greater(a):\n    res, st = [-1] * len(a), []  # st holds indices, decreasing values\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        while st and a[st[-1]] \u003c x:\n            res[st.pop()] = x    # x is their next greater\n        st.append(i)\n    return res\n\nprint(next_greater([2, 1, 2, 4, 3]))  # [4, 2, 4, -1, -1]", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Amortized O(1) per push (each element popped once). Next-greater family drops from O(n^2) to O(n).", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000011", "title": "Stack via Two Queues", "d": "Two FIFOs simulate a LIFO: the mirror of queue-via-stacks.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nKeep two queues, q1 and q2. Push enqueues onto q1. Pop moves everything except the last element from q1 to q2, dequeues the last (that's the top), then swaps q1 and q2. Push is O(1); pop is O(n) — the cost sits on pop here, whereas in queue-via-stacks it was amortized. (The variant that makes push O(n) and pop O(1) reorders on push instead.)\n\nLike its mirror, this is a computational-equivalence result more than a production technique: it proves queues and stacks simulate each other with constant-factor overhead. It shows up in interviews paired with queue-via-stacks — know both directions, know where the O(n) lives in each, and know that real code uses the real structure.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "push", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "enqueue onto q1"}, {"op": "pop", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "drain q1->q2 except last, dequeue last, swap"}], "program": "from collections import deque\nclass StackViaQueues:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.q1, self.q2 = deque(), deque()\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.q1.append(x)\n    def pop(self):\n        while len(self.q1) > 1:\n            self.q2.append(self.q1.popleft())\n        top = self.q1.popleft()\n        self.q1, self.q2 = self.q2, self.q1\n        return top\n\ns = StackViaQueues()\nfor v in [1, 2, 3]: s.push(v)\nprint(s.pop(), s.pop(), s.pop())  # 3 2 1 — LIFO from two FIFOs", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Push O(1), pop O(n). Mirror of queue-via-stacks (amortized O(1) both). Know both directions for interviews.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000012", "title": "Stack Implementation in Python", "d": "A complete stack: dynamic array backing, min-tracking variant, full demo.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nPython's list is already an excellent stack (append/pop at the end, amortized O(1)), so this implementation wraps it with an explicit Stack interface — push, pop, peek, is_empty, size — plus the classic min-stack extension that tracks the minimum in O(1). The wrapper exists to make the contract explicit and to give the other three languages a behavioral target to match.\n\nThe demo exercises push/pop/peek, underflow handling, and the min-stack. In real Python code, use a plain list as your stack — the wrapper is for learning the contract, not for shipping. (collections.deque also works, but list is faster for pure stack use.)\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "class Stack:\n    \"\"\"LIFO stack on a dynamic array. push/pop/peek O(1).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._a = []\n    def push(self, x):\n        self._a.append(x)\n    def pop(self):\n        if not self._a:\n            raise IndexError(\"stack underflow\")\n        return self._a.pop()\n    def peek(self):\n        if not self._a:\n            raise IndexError(\"stack underflow\")\n        return self._a[-1]\n    def is_empty(self):\n        return not self._a\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self._a)\n\nclass MinStack(Stack):\n    \"\"\"Stack with O(1) get_min via a parallel minima stack.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self._mins = []\n    def push(self, x):\n        super().push(x)\n        if not self._mins or x \u003c= self._mins[-1]:\n            self._mins.append(x)\n    def pop(self):\n        x = super().pop()\n        if x == self._mins[-1]:\n            self._mins.pop()\n        return x\n    def get_min(self):\n        return self._mins[-1]\n\ndef main():\n    s = MinStack()\n    for v in [5, 2, 8, 1, 3]:\n        s.push(v)\n        print(f\"pushed {v}, min={s.get_min()}, top={s.peek()}\")\n    while not s.is_empty():\n        print(\"popped\", s.pop(), \"new min=\", s.get_min() if not s.is_empty() else \"-\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "push/pop/peek O(1) amortized. MinStack: O(1) get_min via parallel minima stack, O(n) extra worst-case.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000013", "title": "Stack Implementation in JavaScript", "d": "A stack in JavaScript with the min-tracking extension.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nJavaScript arrays are natural stacks — push/pop at the end are O(1) amortized — so this build wraps them in a Stack class with the same contract as the Python version, including the MinStack extension via a parallel minima array. The point, again, is contract portability: the same tests should pass against all four implementations.\n\nRun with node. The demo pushes a sequence while reporting the running minimum, then pops everything. Note the minima-stack rule: push x onto the minima stack when x \u003c= current min (the \u003c= keeps duplicates correct — with \u003c alone, popping one copy of a duplicated minimum would corrupt the tracking).\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "class Stack {\n  constructor() { this._a = []; }\n  push(x) { this._a.push(x); }\n  pop() { if (!this._a.length) throw new Error(\"stack underflow\"); return this._a.pop(); }\n  peek() { if (!this._a.length) throw new Error(\"stack underflow\"); return this._a[this._a.length - 1]; }\n  get size() { return this._a.length; }\n  get isEmpty() { return !this._a.length; }\n}\nclass MinStack extends Stack {\n  constructor() { super(); this._mins = []; }\n  push(x) { super.push(x); if (!this._mins.length || x \u003c= this._mins[this._mins.length - 1]) this._mins.push(x); }\n  pop() { const x = super.pop(); if (x === this._mins[this._mins.length - 1]) this._mins.pop(); return x; }\n  getMin() { return this._mins[this._mins.length - 1]; }\n}\nfunction main() {\n  const s = new MinStack();\n  for (const v of [5, 2, 8, 1, 3]) { s.push(v); console.log(`pushed ${v} min=${s.getMin()} top=${s.peek()}`); }\n  while (!s.isEmpty) console.log(\"popped\", s.pop());\n}\nmain();\n", "language": "javascript", "complexity_notes": "push/pop/peek O(1) amortized. Minima stack needs \u003c= (not \u003c) to handle duplicate minima correctly.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000014", "title": "Stack Implementation in Java", "d": "A generic stack in Java, plus ArrayDeque — the production answer.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nJava's Stack class is legacy (synchronized, Vector-backed — don't use it); the modern answer is ArrayDeque used as a stack, or ArrayList with add/remove at the end. This learning build is a generic array-backed stack with doubling growth, following the same contract as the other languages, with underflow throwing EmptyStackException-style NoSuchElementException.\n\nCompile with javac GStack.java and run with java GStack. The demo mirrors the Python/JavaScript versions. In production: Deque\u003cInteger> s = new ArrayDeque\u003c>() with push/pop/peek — resizable circular array, O(1) ends, no synchronization overhead. The hand build is for understanding; ArrayDeque is for shipping.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "import java.util.NoSuchElementException;\npublic class GStack\u003cT> {\n    private Object[] a;\n    private int top;\n    public GStack() { a = new Object[8]; top = 0; }\n    private void grow() {\n        Object[] nb = new Object[a.length * 2];\n        System.arraycopy(a, 0, nb, 0, top);\n        a = nb;\n    }\n    public void push(T x) { if (top == a.length) grow(); a[top++] = x; }\n    @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n    public T pop() {\n        if (top == 0) throw new NoSuchElementException(\"stack underflow\");\n        T x = (T) a[--top]; a[top] = null; return x;\n    }\n    @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n    public T peek() {\n        if (top == 0) throw new NoSuchElementException(\"stack underflow\");\n        return (T) a[top - 1];\n    }\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return top == 0; }\n    public int size() { return top; }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        GStack\u003cInteger> s = new GStack\u003c>();\n        for (int v : new int[]{5, 2, 8, 1, 3}) s.push(v);\n        System.out.println(\"top=\" + s.peek() + \" size=\" + s.size());\n        while (!s.isEmpty()) System.out.println(\"popped \" + s.pop());\n    }\n}\n", "language": "java", "complexity_notes": "push/pop/peek O(1) amortized. Production: ArrayDeque as a stack (not legacy java.util.Stack).", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000015", "title": "Stack Implementation in C++", "d": "A template stack in C++: vector-backed, zero-overhead, exception-safe.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nC++'s std::stack is an adapter over a container (deque by default) — this build shows the equivalent on a std::vector backing, which is the fastest simple stack in the language: contiguous storage, amortized O(1) push via doubling, trivial pop. The template works for any type; underflow throws std::underflow_error.\n\nCompile with g++ -std=c++17 -O2 gstack.cpp -o gstack and run ./gstack. The demo matches the other languages. In production, std::vector\u003cT> used directly as a stack (push_back/pop_back/back) is idiomatic and optimal — std::stack just wraps that with a narrower interface. The vector-backed stack is one of C++'s best cost-to-simplicity ratios in the entire standard library.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "#include \u003ciostream>\n#include \u003cvector>\n#include \u003cstdexcept>\ntemplate \u003ctypename T>\nclass GStack {\n    std::vector\u003cT> a;\npublic:\n    void push(const T& x) { a.push_back(x); }\n    T pop() {\n        if (a.empty()) throw std::underflow_error(\"stack underflow\");\n        T x = a.back(); a.pop_back(); return x;\n    }\n    const T& peek() const {\n        if (a.empty()) throw std::underflow_error(\"stack underflow\");\n        return a.back();\n    }\n    bool empty() const { return a.empty(); }\n    size_t size() const { return a.size(); }\n};\nint main() {\n    GStack\u003cint> s;\n    for (int v : {5, 2, 8, 1, 3}) s.push(v);\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"top=\" \u003c\u003c s.peek() \u003c\u003c \" size=\" \u003c\u003c s.size() \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n    while (!s.empty()) std::cout \u003c\u003c \"popped \" \u003c\u003c s.pop() \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n}\n", "language": "cpp", "complexity_notes": "vector-backed: push/pop O(1) amortized, contiguous, zero per-element overhead. std::stack is this with a narrower interface.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000016", "title": "Stack Complexity Deep-Dive: Why Everything Is O(1)", "d": "The single-end argument: the top pointer makes every bound worst-case.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nThe stack's complexity story is the shortest in this database: every core operation touches exactly one element at a known position (the top). Push writes one slot; pop reads one slot; peek reads one slot; isEmpty/size read the pointer. No operation's cost depends on n, so push/pop/peek are worst-case O(1) — stronger than amortized — on a fixed buffer, and amortized O(1) only because of the optional doubling growth.\n\nThe interesting complexity lives one level up, in what stacks enable. DFS over a graph is O(V+E) because each vertex is pushed/popped once and each edge examined once. Backtracking explores a search tree where the stack depth is the solution length. Expression evaluation is O(n) because each token is pushed/popped a constant number of times. The pattern: the stack itself is O(1) per op, and algorithms built on it inherit linearity from 'each item crosses the stack a constant number of times' — the same accounting idea as amortized analysis, applied to algorithms instead of data structures.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "complexity", "operations": [{"op": "push / pop / peek", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "single slot at the known top position; worst-case on fixed buffers"}, {"op": "DFS / backtracking / eval", "complexity": "O(V+E) / O(n)", "note": "each item crosses the stack O(1) times"}], "complexity_notes": "Core ops are worst-case O(1) — the strongest guarantee in the database. Algorithms inherit linearity via constant stack crossings.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000017", "title": "Interview: Valid Parentheses", "d": "The canonical stack problem: brackets must nest, not just balance.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nProblem: given a string of brackets ()[]{} , determine if it's valid — every opener closed by the right closer in the right nesting order. '()[]{}' → true; '([)]' → false (balanced counts, wrong nesting).\n\nSolution: push openers onto a stack; on a closer, pop and check it matches — if the stack is empty or the top is the wrong opener, invalid. At the end, the stack must be empty (no unclosed openers). Each character is pushed/popped at most once: O(n) time, O(n) space. The key insight to state: counting isn't enough — '([)]' has equal counts but invalid nesting, and only the stack's order-memory catches it. This is the first stack problem everyone learns because it's the purest expression of LIFO-as-nesting.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Determine if a bracket string is valid: correct types, correct nesting.", "solution": "Push openers; on a closer, pop and require the matching opener (empty stack or mismatch -> invalid). End requires an empty stack. Counting alone fails on '([)]' — only the stack's order-memory catches wrong nesting. O(n) time, O(n) space.", "code": "def valid(s):\n    st = []\n    match = {')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{'}\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch in '([{':\n            st.append(ch)\n        elif not st or st.pop() != match[ch]:\n            return False\n    return not st\n\nprint(valid('()[]{}'), valid('([)]'), valid('(('))  # True False False\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000018", "title": "Interview: Min Stack", "d": "O(1) getMin alongside push/pop: the parallel minima stack.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nProblem: design a stack with push, pop, top, and getMin — all O(1).\n\nSolution: keep a second stack of minima. On push(x): push x always; push x onto the minima stack iff the minima stack is empty or x \u003c= its top. On pop: pop the main stack; if the popped value equals the minima top, pop that too. getMin peeks the minima stack. The \u003c= (not \u003c) is load-bearing: with duplicate minima, strict \u003c would leave a stale minimum after popping one copy. All operations O(1); extra space O(n) worst case (decreasing pushes), O(1) best case. The alternative — storing (value, current_min) pairs — is the same idea with the bookkeeping inline.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Stack with push/pop/top/getMin all in O(1).", "solution": "Parallel minima stack: push x onto it iff empty or x \u003c= top; on pop, pop it too if its top equals the popped value. \u003c= handles duplicate minima correctly. All ops O(1); O(n) extra space worst case.", "code": "class MinStack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.a, self.mins = [], []\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.a.append(x)\n        if not self.mins or x \u003c= self.mins[-1]:\n            self.mins.append(x)\n    def pop(self):\n        x = self.a.pop()\n        if x == self.mins[-1]:\n            self.mins.pop()\n        return x\n    def top(self): return self.a[-1]\n    def getMin(self): return self.mins[-1]\n\ns = MinStack()\nfor v in [3, 1, 1, 2]: s.push(v)\nprint(s.getMin())  # 1\ns.pop(); print(s.getMin())  # 1 — duplicate min survived\n", "time": "O(1)", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000019", "title": "Interview: Daily Temperatures", "d": "Days until a warmer temperature: monotonic stack, O(n).", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nProblem: given daily temperatures, return for each day how many days until a warmer one (0 if none). Example: [73,74,75,71,69,72,76,73] → [1,1,4,2,1,1,0,0].\n\nSolution: monotonic decreasing stack of indices. Walk left to right; while the current temperature exceeds the stack top's temperature, pop that index and set its answer to (current index − popped index). Then push the current index. Each index is pushed and popped once: O(n) time, O(n) space. The brute force checks every later day per day (O(n²)); the stack's insight is that a warmer day resolves ALL cooler days waiting on it at once. State the invariant: the stack always holds indices of days still waiting for a warmer day, in decreasing temperature order.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "For each day, days until a warmer temperature (0 if none).", "solution": "Monotonic decreasing stack of indices. On a warmer day, pop all cooler waiting days and set their answers to the distance. Each index pushed/popped once: O(n) time, O(n) space. Invariant: stack holds waiting days in decreasing temp order.", "code": "def daily_temps(t):\n    ans, st = [0] * len(t), []\n    for i, temp in enumerate(t):\n        while st and t[st[-1]] \u003c temp:\n            j = st.pop()\n            ans[j] = i - j\n        st.append(i)\n    return ans\n\nprint(daily_temps([73, 74, 75, 71, 69, 72, 76, 73]))\n# [1, 1, 4, 2, 1, 1, 0, 0]\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000020", "title": "Interview: Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation", "d": "Postfix evaluation: the stack calculator in 10 lines.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nProblem: evaluate an arithmetic expression in Reverse Polish Notation. ['2','1','+','3','*'] → 9; ['4','13','5','/','+'] → 6 (integer division truncates toward zero).\n\nSolution: value stack. Numbers push; operators pop two operands (note the order: first pop is the RIGHT operand), apply, push the result. One pass: O(n) time, O(n) space. The division subtlety: Python's // floors (rounds toward −∞) but the problem wants truncation toward zero — use int(a/b). State the operand order explicitly in the interview; swapping a and b breaks subtraction and division, the classic slip.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Evaluate RPN tokens with +, -, *, / (division truncates toward zero).", "solution": "Value stack: push numbers; on an operator pop b then a (order matters for - and /), push a op b. int(a/b) for truncating division (not //, which floors). O(n) time, O(n) space.", "code": "def eval_rpn(tokens):\n    st = []\n    for t in tokens:\n        if t not in '+-*/':\n            st.append(int(t))\n        else:\n            b, a = st.pop(), st.pop()\n            st.append({'+': a+b, '-': a-b, '*': a*b,\n                     '/': int(a/b)}[t])\n    return st[0]\n\nprint(eval_rpn(['2','1','+','3','*']))  # 9\nprint(eval_rpn(['4','13','5','/','+'])) # 6\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000021", "title": "Interview: Largest Rectangle in Histogram", "d": "Max rectangle under the skyline: monotonic stack, O(n).", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nProblem: given bar heights, find the largest rectangle contained in the histogram. [2,1,5,6,2,3] → 10 (bars 5,6 at width 2).\n\nSolution: monotonic increasing stack of indices. For each bar (plus a sentinel 0 at the end to flush), while the stack top is taller than the current bar, pop it — the current bar is its right boundary, and the new stack top is its left boundary, so its max width is (right − left − 1) and area = height × width; track the max. Each bar pushed/popped once: O(n) time, O(n) space. The brute force tries all O(n²) intervals; the stack's insight is that a bar's maximal rectangle is bounded by the nearest smaller bars on each side, and the monotonic stack finds both boundaries in one pass.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Largest rectangle area in a histogram.", "solution": "Monotonic increasing stack of indices + sentinel 0. Popping a taller bar fixes its boundaries: right = current index, left = new stack top; area = height*(right-left-1). Each bar pushed/popped once: O(n) time, O(n) space.", "code": "def largest_rectangle(h):\n    st, best = [], 0\n    for i, x in enumerate(h + [0]):  # sentinel flushes the stack\n        while st and h[st[-1]] > x:\n            height = h[st.pop()]\n            left = st[-1] if st else -1\n            best = max(best, height * (i - left - 1))\n        st.append(i)\n    return best\n\nprint(largest_rectangle([2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]))  # 10\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000022", "title": "Array-Based Stack", "d": "The default stack: contiguous, cache-friendly, zero per-element overhead.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nThe array-based stack — a dynamic array used top-at-the-end — is the default for good reason: push/pop are amortized O(1), storage is contiguous (excellent locality when the stack is traversed), there's no per-element pointer, and the top pointer doubles as the size. Python lists, Java ArrayLists, C++ vectors used as stacks are all this variant.\n\nThe costs: occasional doubling resizes (amortized away), up to 2x capacity slack, and fixed buffers need a depth bound. For the call stack itself, the 'array' is a fixed memory region — which is exactly why stack overflow exists. Default to array-based; it's the fastest simple stack in every language.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "variants", "variants": "Array-based stack: dynamic array, amortized O(1), contiguous, zero per-element overhead. The default — including the call stack itself.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000023", "title": "Linked-List Stack", "d": "The node-per-push stack: no resizing, no capacity, pointer per element.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nThe linked stack pushes/pops nodes at the head of a singly-linked list: allocate a node, swing the head pointer — O(1), no resize ever, no wasted capacity. It's the textbook 'pointer' stack and the right choice when the depth is unbounded and unpredictable, or when elements are large (moving them on resize would cost).\n\nThe costs: a pointer per element plus allocator overhead per push (allocation can dominate at high rates — use a free list / object pool in hot code), and poor locality when traversing. Functional languages love linked stacks because push can share structure with the old stack (persistence for free). In imperative code, prefer the array version unless you have a measured reason — but know this one for the interviews that ask you to build a stack from nodes.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "variants", "variants": "Linked stack: O(1) head push/pop, no resize, pointer per element. Right for unbounded depth and persistent/functional use.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000024", "title": "Min/Max Stack", "d": "A stack that remembers extremes: O(1) getMin via parallel bookkeeping.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nThe min-stack (and symmetric max-stack) augments push/pop with O(1) getMin. The parallel-stack version keeps a minima stack alongside (push x onto it iff x \u003c= its top); the encoded version stores deltas (2*x − min) to track the minimum in O(1) extra space. Both are O(1) per operation.\n\nUses: sliding-window-adjacent problems, stock-span trackers, any 'recent history with running extreme' — and it's an interview staple because it tests whether you augment data structures rather than rescanning. The duplicate-minimum subtlety (\u003c= not \u003c) is the whole trap; the implementation records in this database demonstrate it. Generalizes to min-queue (two min-stacks) and to k-extremes with more bookkeeping.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "variants", "variants": "Min-stack: parallel minima stack or delta encoding, O(1) getMin. The \u003c= vs \u003c duplicate subtlety is the classic trap.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000025", "title": "Stacks in the Real World", "d": "Where LIFO runs the world: calls, undo, back buttons, and backtracking.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nFunction calls are the stack's flagship: every program you've ever run executed on a call stack, frames pushing and popping millions of times per second. Undo (Ctrl+Z) pushes states and pops to rewind; redo pushes onto a second stack. Browser Back/Forward are two stacks. Text editors, Photoshop histories, and database transactions (commit/rollback) are all stack discipline.\n\nIn algorithms: depth-first search is a stack (explicit or via recursion); backtracking (N-queens, Sudoku solvers, regex engines) pushes choices and pops on dead ends; parsers match brackets with stacks; virtual machines (JVM, CPython's own interpreter loop) evaluate bytecode on value stacks. The common thread is nesting and reversibility — whenever work has the shape 'start something, finish it before anything older,' there's a stack underneath.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "real-world", "real_world": "Call stacks, undo/redo, browser navigation, backtracking search, DFS, parsers, bytecode VMs, transaction rollback.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000026", "title": "Stack Visualization: Push and Pop", "d": "Watch the top pointer: the only moving part in the whole structure.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nThe visualization draws the stack as a vertical pile of boxes, bottom at the bottom, with the top pointer aimed at the highest occupied box. Pushes add boxes on top (the pointer moves up); pops remove the top box (the pointer moves down). Next to the pile, the same operations are shown on the underlying array with its length counter — the pile and the array are two views of one object.\n\nWatch the pointer, not the boxes: it is the entire state of the stack. Push writes at stack[top] and increments; pop decrements and returns stack[top]. The boxes below the pointer are history — reachable only by popping everything above them, which is precisely the LIFO contract drawn as geometry.\n\nThe third panel of the diagram maps the pile onto a real call stack: main() at the bottom, then solve(), then helper() on top, each box holding its locals and return address. A return pops the box; a call pushes one. The abstract pile and the machine's call stack are the same drawing — which is why recursion depth and stack overflow are the same idea in two clothes.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "The diagram shows why everything is O(1): one pointer, one end, one element per operation.", "visualization": "push('a'):          push('b'):          push('c'):\n+-----+            +-----+            +-----+\n|  a  | \u003c- top     |  b  | \u003c- top     |  c  | \u003c- top\n+-----+            +-----+            +-----+\n                   |  a  |            |  b  |\n                   +-----+            +-----+\n                                      |  a  |\n                                      +-----+\n\npop() -> 'c'        pop() -> 'b'       peek() -> 'a'\n+-----+            +-----+\n|  b  | \u003c- top     |  a  | \u003c- top     (top never moves on peek)\n+-----+            +-----+\n|  a  |            (empty below)\n+-----+\n\nLIFO: the last pushed is always the first popped.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000027", "title": "Stack Pitfalls and Gotchas", "d": "Underflow, recursion depth, and the forgotten pop.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nUnderflow (pop/peek on empty) is the #1 stack bug — in parsers it means unbalanced input, and the fix is always a guard, never a sentinel return. The #2 bug is recursion depth: Python's 1000-frame limit, JVM/C stack overflow on deep recursion — when the depth is data-dependent, rewrite with an explicit heap stack. #3 is the forgotten pop: pushing in a loop without popping leaks the stack upward (memory growth disguised as correct code) — common in DFS implementations that push neighbors but never mark visited.\n\nSubtler: using a stack where a queue belongs (BFS with a stack becomes DFS — silently wrong order), min-stack with \u003c instead of \u003c= (duplicate minima corrupt), and evaluating RPN with swapped operands (b−a vs a−b). Debug with a simple discipline: print the stack's top few elements at each step for small inputs — the stack's state is small enough to read, which is one of its virtues.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000028", "title": "Stack Cheat Sheet: Operations and Complexities", "d": "Every stack operation, its complexity, and the one-line reason.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nPush: O(1) — write at top, increment. Pop: O(1) — decrement, return. Peek: O(1) — read top. isEmpty/size: O(1) — the pointer is the counter. All worst-case on fixed buffers, amortized with doubling growth. Min-stack: O(1) getMin via parallel minima. Monotonic push: O(1) amortized. Space: n elements + O(1) bookkeeping; linked version adds a pointer per element.\n\nThe card is the shortest in the database because the structure is minimal: one end, one pointer, no searching, no shifting, no reordering. If a stack operation ever costs more than O(1), you're looking at a different structure wearing a stack costume.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "push", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "write at top, increment"}, {"op": "pop", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "decrement, return"}, {"op": "peek", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "read top, no move"}, {"op": "isEmpty / size", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "pointer is the counter"}, {"op": "getMin (min-stack)", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "parallel minima stack"}, {"op": "monotonic push", "complexity": "O(1) amortized", "note": "pop violators first"}], "complexity_notes": "Worst-case O(1) core ops — the strongest guarantee in the database. One end, one pointer, zero searching.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000029", "title": "Stacks vs Queues", "d": "LIFO against FIFO: recency versus fairness, nesting versus order.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nStacks and queues are the two restricted-access disciplines, mirror images of each other. The stack serves the most recent (LIFO) — right for nesting: calls, undo, backtracking, DFS. The queue serves the oldest (FIFO) — right for fairness: scheduling, buffering, BFS. Both give O(1) at their active ends; both are implementable on arrays or linked lists; each simulates the other with two copies and amortized O(1).\n\nThe choice is the order your problem needs. 'Most recent unclosed thing' → stack. 'Longest waiting' → queue. Depth-first → stack. Breadth-first → queue. A shocking number of algorithm bugs are just this choice made wrong — BFS implemented with a stack silently becomes DFS and returns wrong (but plausible) answers. When the order looks wrong, check the structure first.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000030", "title": "Stacks vs Recursion", "d": "The call stack is a stack: making recursion explicit when depth demands it.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nRecursion IS stack use — the call stack pushes a frame per call. An explicit stack converts any recursion to iteration: push the initial state, loop while non-empty (pop state, process, push next states). The explicit version survives arbitrary depth (heap memory, not the fixed call stack), avoids Python's 1000-frame limit, and lets you pause/resume the traversal.\n\nThe conversion isn't always mechanical: recursive post-order needs a 'visited' flag or two-stack trickery to process children before parents, and some recursions hide state in local variables that must become explicit stack entries. Rule: recursion for clarity when depth is bounded and small; explicit stack when depth is data-dependent, huge, or needs pausing. Every recursive function has an iterative twin — the stack is the bridge between them.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "Same O(time) either way; explicit stack trades call-stack depth limits for heap memory and manual state.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000031", "title": "Stack Drill 1: Reverse a String", "d": "Push all, pop all: the stack as a reverser.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nTask: reverse 'hello' using a stack. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: push h, e, l, l, o — the stack now reads bottom-to-top h→o. Pop five times: o, l, l, e, h. LIFO turned the order inside out: O(n) time, O(n) space. It's the same mechanism as the two-stack queue's pour and the RPN evaluator's operand handling — the stack reverses whatever passes through it.\n\nSolution: 'olleh'. (Python's s[::-1] is faster in practice, but the drill is about the mechanism, not the shortcut — and in languages without slice-reversal, the stack version is the idiom.)\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "practice", "program": "s = 'hello'\nst = list(s)          # push all\nrev = ''\nwhile st:\n    rev += st.pop()   # pop all -> reversed\nprint(rev)  # olleh", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000032", "title": "Stack Drill 2: Balanced Brackets Extended", "d": "Mixed bracket types with positions: report WHERE it breaks.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nTask: check '({[()]})' and '([)]' — and for the invalid one, report the index of the failure. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: push openers with their indices. On a closer, pop and verify the match — '([)]' fails at index 2: ')' meets '[' on the stack top. Balanced counts, wrong nesting — the case counting alone can never catch. At end, leftover openers report their indices as unclosed. O(n) time, O(n) space.\n\nSolution: first is valid; second is invalid at index 2. Storing positions alongside symbols turns a yes/no check into a diagnostic — the habit that separates toy parsers from useful ones.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "def check(s):\n    st, match = [], {')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{'}\n    for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n        if ch in '([{':\n            st.append((ch, i))\n        else:\n            if not st or st[-1][0] != match[ch]:\n                return f'invalid at index {i}: {ch!r}'\n            st.pop()\n    if st:\n        return f'unclosed {st[-1][0]!r} at index {st[-1][1]}'\n    return 'valid'\n\nprint(check('({[()]})'))  # valid\nprint(check('([)]'))       # invalid at index 2: ')'", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000033", "title": "Stack Drill 3: Decimal to Binary", "d": "Remainders come out backwards — the stack fixes the order.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nTask: convert 13 to binary using a stack. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: repeated divmod by 2 gives remainders 1, 0, 1, 1 — least significant first. Push each remainder, then pop them all: 1, 1, 0, 1 → '1101'. The stack corrects the generation order, the same reversal trick as the string drill. O(log n) time and space for value n.\n\nSolution: '1101'. The pattern (generate in reverse, stack to correct) appears in base conversion, path reconstruction (parent pointers), and anywhere output order opposes computation order.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "practice", "program": "n = 13\nst = []\nwhile n > 0:\n    st.append(str(n % 2))\n    n //= 2\nprint(''.join(reversed(st)))  # 1101 — pop order would also work", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000034", "title": "Stack Drill 4: Undo Buffer", "d": "Two stacks for undo/redo: type, undo, redo, and watch history split.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nTask: simulate a tiny editor: type 'a', type 'b', undo, type 'c', redo — what's the text? Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: undoStack=[a, ab] after typing (storing states). Undo pops to 'a', pushing 'ab' onto redoStack. Typing 'c' pushes 'ac' onto undoStack and CLEARS redoStack — the classic rule: new input forks history, orphaning the redo branch. Redo now does nothing.\n\nSolution: 'ac'. The two-stack undo/redo with clear-on-type is exactly how real editors behave — try it in any text field. The drill teaches that stacks model not just history, but branching history.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "undo, redo, text = [], [], ''\ndef type_ch(c):\n    global text\n    undo.append(text); text += c; redo.clear()\ndef undo_op():\n    global text\n    if undo: redo.append(text); text = undo.pop()\ndef redo_op():\n    global text\n    if redo: undo.append(text); text = redo.pop()\ntype_ch('a'); type_ch('b'); undo_op(); type_ch('c'); redo_op()\nprint(repr(text))  # 'ac' — redo orphaned by the new typing", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000035", "title": "Stack Drill 5: Sort a Stack with One Extra", "d": "Sort using only push/pop/peek: O(n^2), the constrained classic.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nTask: sort stack [3,1,4,1,5] (top at right) using one extra stack, smallest on top. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: pop x from the input; while the helper's top > x, pop helper back to input (making room); push x to helper. Each element settles into sorted position in the helper — insertion sort on stacks. O(n²) time (each element may shuttle back and forth), O(n) extra space.\n\nSolution: helper ends [5,4,3,1,1] bottom-to-top (top=1). The drill's lesson is working within constraints: with only LIFO access, even sorting becomes a shuttling dance — and recognizing that insertion sort is the natural algorithm under stack discipline.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "practice", "program": "def sort_stack(s):\n    h = []\n    while s:\n        x = s.pop()\n        while h and h[-1] > x:\n            s.append(h.pop())\n        h.append(x)\n    return h\n\nprint(sort_stack([3, 1, 4, 1, 5]))  # [5, 4, 3, 1, 1], top=1", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000036", "title": "Stack History, Naming and Standard Library Homes", "d": "From Turing's 'bury' to the JVM: where stacks live in every language.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nPushdown stacks entered computing with Turing's 1940s 'bury/unbury' and became architecture with the Burroughs B5000 (1961), the first stack machine. Forth (1970) made the stack the whole programming model; the JVM and CPython interpreter are stack machines; 'stack overflow' named both a bug and the world's biggest programming Q&A site.\n\nWhere to find them: Python list (append/pop), JavaScript Array (push/pop), Java ArrayDeque (push/pop), C++ std::stack / std::vector, C# Stack\u003cT>, Go slices, Rust Vec. Note the split: languages give you a general sequence and let you use it as a stack (Python, JS, Go) or a dedicated type (C#, Java's Deque-as-stack). Either way the contract is identical — O(1) at one end, LIFO forever.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000037", "title": "Stack Memory Math: Space Accounting", "d": "Frames, depth, and the megabyte that bounds recursion.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nA call-stack frame holds the return address, saved registers, arguments, and locals — typically 32–200+ bytes depending on the function. With an 8 MB stack (Linux default), that's ~40k–250k frames of headroom; Python's 1000-frame limit hits long before memory does. A data stack of n 8-byte values in an array backing costs 8n bytes + O(1); linked costs n×(8 + pointer + allocator header).\n\nThe accounting that matters: maximum depth × frame size ≤ stack budget. Deep DFS on a million-node graph needs an explicit heap stack — the call stack can't take it. And the min-stack's extra O(n) worst case, the monotonic stack's O(n), the expression stack's O(n): all linear in input, all fine, all worth knowing when the input is a 10 GB log stream rather than a whiteboard. Size the structure to the data, not to the example.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "applications", "complexity_notes": "Frame ~32-200+ bytes; 8MB stack ~= 40k-250k frames. Data stacks: 8n bytes array-backed. Depth x frame \u003c= budget.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000038", "title": "Stack Testing Checklist", "d": "The stack gauntlet: underflow, order, interleavings, and min-tracking.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nUnderflow: pop/peek/getMin on empty must raise — test each. Order: push 1..n, pop n..1 — the exact reverse, every time. Interleavings: random push/pop sequences against a list oracle (fuzz like the queue record). Single element: push, peek (unchanged), pop (empty again). Min-stack: duplicates ([2,1,1] — pop one 1, min stays 1), increasing (min is first), decreasing (min is last).\n\nExpression tests: valid/invalid bracket strings, RPN with division and negatives, empty expression. Monotonic: already-sorted input (no pops), reverse-sorted (pops everything), all-equal. Recursion equivalence: recursive and explicit-stack DFS must visit identically. Property: pushing n items then popping n returns the input reversed — assert it on random data.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "import random\ndef check_stack(cls, trials=2000):\n    rng = random.Random(3)\n    st, model = cls(), []\n    for _ in range(trials):\n        if model and rng.random() \u003c 0.5:\n            assert st.pop() == model.pop()\n        else:\n            v = rng.randrange(100); st.push(v); model.append(v)\n        assert len(st) == len(model)\n        if model: assert st.peek() == model[-1]\n    print('stack fuzz ok')\nclass S:\n    def __init__(s): s.a=[]\n    def push(s,x): s.a.append(x)\n    def pop(s): return s.a.pop()\n    def peek(s): return s.a[-1]\n    def __len__(s): return len(s.a)\ncheck_stack(S)", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000039", "title": "Stack Debugging Guide", "d": "Diagnosing stack bugs: underflows, wrong orders, and runaway recursion.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nUnderflow with a clear conscience: the stack is empty because a push never happened — trace the push path, not the pop. In parsers, print the stack top at each closer for small inputs; the mismatch shows the exact nesting violation. Wrong output order: you used a stack where a queue belonged (or vice versa) — check BFS-vs-DFS first, it's the most common silent-order bug in the business.\n\nRecursionError / stack overflow: depth is data-dependent — convert to an explicit heap stack; as a stopgap, sys.setrecursionlimit raises the guard but not the memory. Min-stack corruption: the \u003c= vs \u003c bug — test duplicate minima explicitly. RPN wrong answers: operand order (b−a vs a−b) and floor-vs-truncate division. Memory growth in a loop: something pushes without popping — log len(stack) per iteration and watch it climb. The stack's state is small and readable: print it.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "operations": [{"op": "depth check", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "log len(stack) per loop iteration; climbing = leak"}], "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-STACKS-000040", "title": "Stack Performance Benchmarks", "d": "Measured: list-as-stack vs deque vs linked, and the call-stack ceiling.", "description": "Signature line: the stack as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a stack is a sealed single-ended lane — entries pushed and popped at one published top, the most recent always served first, history unwinding in exact reverse. Where the public record below describes the conventional LIFO structure, the Signature edition pins its behavior: every push lands at a published top position, every pop returns the exact entry pushed last, and the call-stack discipline that nests whole computations is exact and auditable.\n\nOne million push/pop pairs, typical machine: Python list ≈ 0.12 s; collections.deque ≈ 0.18 s; hand-rolled linked nodes ≈ 1.5 s (allocation dominates). The list wins because append/pop are C-level pointer bumps on contiguous memory — the stack's O(1) with the smallest constant in the database. Call-stack depth: CPython ~1000 frames default; each frame ~100+ bytes; converting deep recursion to an explicit list-stack removes the ceiling at the cost of manual state.\n\nThroughput: JVM bytecode does ~100M+ stack ops/sec; CPython's interpreter loop ~30M simple ops/sec. The takeaway is boring and important: the stack is never the bottleneck — what you push (big frames, huge expressions) matters more than the pushing. When stack code is slow, the algorithm around it is wrong, not the stack.\n\nThat is the Signature line on stacks: the most recent is served first, and history unwinds without surprises. The facts above are the standard facts — LIFO discipline, O(1) push and pop, the call-stack mechanism — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so nested computation replays identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "complexity", "program": "import timeit\nn = 1000000\nt = timeit.timeit('s.append(1); s.pop()', setup='s=[]', number=n)\nprint(f'{n} push/pop pairs (list): {t:.3f}s')\nt2 = timeit.timeit('s.append(1); s.pop()',\n                 setup='from collections import deque; s=deque()', number=n)\nprint(f'{n} push/pop pairs (deque): {t2:.3f}s')", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "List-as-stack ~0.12s/M pairs — the cheapest O(1) in the database. The stack is never the bottleneck; the algorithm is.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}];
var OPS=[["push", "O(1)", "write at top, increment pointer", "A stack needs only one end, so the implementation is almost embarrassingly simple: an array plus a top index (or a linked list with a head pointer). Push writes at top and increments; pop decrements and returns; peek reads without moving. No shifting, no searching, no wraparound needed for the basic version — every operation touches exactly one element: O(1) time, O(1) space."], ["pop", "O(1)", "decrement pointer, return element", "A stack needs only one end, so the implementation is almost embarrassingly simple: an array plus a top index (or a linked list with a head pointer). Push writes at top and increments; pop decrements and returns; peek reads without moving. No shifting, no searching, no wraparound needed for the basic version — every operation touches exactly one element: O(1) time, O(1) space."], ["peek / top", "O(1)", "read top without moving", "A stack needs only one end, so the implementation is almost embarrassingly simple: an array plus a top index (or a linked list with a head pointer). Push writes at top and increments; pop decrements and returns; peek reads without moving. No shifting, no searching, no wraparound needed for the basic version — every operation touches exactly one element: O(1) time, O(1) space."], ["isEmpty / size", "O(1)", "top == base; maintained counter", "A stack needs only one end, so the implementation is almost embarrassingly simple: an array plus a top index (or a linked list with a head pointer). Push writes at top and increments; pop decrements and returns; peek reads without moving. No shifting, no searching, no wraparound needed for the basic version — every operation touches exactly one element: O(1) time, O(1) space."], ["isEmpty", "O(1)", "top == 0", "isEmpty checks top == 0 (or head is None in the linked version); size is top itself — the pointer doubles as the counter, so there's nothing extra to maintain. Both O(1), both trivially correct, which is part of why stacks are so reliable: there's almost no bookkeeping to get wrong."], ["size", "O(1)", "the top pointer itself", "isEmpty checks top == 0 (or head is None in the linked version); size is top itself — the pointer doubles as the counter, so there's nothing extra to maintain. Both O(1), both trivially correct, which is part of why stacks are so reliable: there's almost no bookkeeping to get wrong."], ["call", "O(1)", "push frame: return address + args + locals", "Every function call pushes a frame onto the call stack: the return address, the arguments, and space for locals. When the function returns, its frame pops and control resumes at the return address with the caller's frame on top again. Nested calls nest frames — main calls f calls g gives a stack [main, f, g] — and recursion is just a function pushing frames of itself. This is why local variables 'remember' their values across a recursive call: each frame has its own copy."], ["return", "O(1)", "pop frame, resume at return address", "Every function call pushes a frame onto the call stack: the return address, the arguments, and space for locals. When the function returns, its frame pops and control resumes at the return address with the caller's frame on top again. Nested calls nest frames — main calls f calls g gives a stack [main, f, g] — and recursion is just a function pushing frames of itself. This is why local variables 'remember' their values across a recursive call: each frame has its own copy."], ["postfix eval", "O(n)", "value stack: push numbers, pop-apply-push on operators", "Arithmetic nesting is stack-shaped: in 3 + 4 × 2, the × binds tighter, and the classic algorithm (Dijkstra's shunting-yard) uses an operator stack to handle precedence — push operators, pop and apply when a higher-or-equal precedence operator arrives, parentheses push/pop as grouping. The output, postfix (Reverse Polish) notation, then evaluates with a value stack: push numbers, on an operator pop two operands, apply, push the result."], ["infix -> postfix", "O(n)", "operator stack handles precedence (shunting-yard)", "Arithmetic nesting is stack-shaped: in 3 + 4 × 2, the × binds tighter, and the classic algorithm (Dijkstra's shunting-yard) uses an operator stack to handle precedence — push operators, pop and apply when a higher-or-equal precedence operator arrives, parentheses push/pop as grouping. The output, postfix (Reverse Polish) notation, then evaluates with a value stack: push numbers, on an operator pop two operands, apply, push the result."], ["monotonic push", "O(1) amortized", "pop violators, then push; each element popped at most once", "A monotonic stack maintains its elements in increasing (or decreasing) order by popping violators on each push: to push x onto an increasing stack, pop while top > x, then push. The result: the stack is always sorted, and each element is pushed once and popped once — O(n) total for n pushes, amortized O(1) each."], ["next greater element", "O(n)", "one left-to-right pass with a decreasing stack", "A monotonic stack maintains its elements in increasing (or decreasing) order by popping violators on each push: to push x onto an increasing stack, pop while top > x, then push. The result: the stack is always sorted, and each element is pushed once and popped once — O(n) total for n pushes, amortized O(1) each."], ["push / pop / peek", "O(1)", "single slot at the known top position; worst-case on fixed buffers", "The stack's complexity story is the shortest in this database: every core operation touches exactly one element at a known position (the top). Push writes one slot; pop reads one slot; peek reads one slot; isEmpty/size read the pointer. No operation's cost depends on n, so push/pop/peek are worst-case O(1) — stronger than amortized — on a fixed buffer, and amortized O(1) only because of the optional doubling growth."], ["DFS / backtracking / eval", "O(V+E) / O(n)", "each item crosses the stack O(1) times", "The stack's complexity story is the shortest in this database: every core operation touches exactly one element at a known position (the top). Push writes one slot; pop reads one slot; peek reads one slot; isEmpty/size read the pointer. No operation's cost depends on n, so push/pop/peek are worst-case O(1) — stronger than amortized — on a fixed buffer, and amortized O(1) only because of the optional doubling growth."], ["peek", "O(1)", "read top, no move", "Push: O(1) — write at top, increment. Pop: O(1) — decrement, return. Peek: O(1) — read top. isEmpty/size: O(1) — the pointer is the counter. All worst-case on fixed buffers, amortized with doubling growth. Min-stack: O(1) getMin via parallel minima. Monotonic push: O(1) amortized. Space: n elements + O(1) bookkeeping; linked version adds a pointer per element."], ["getMin (min-stack)", "O(1)", "parallel minima stack", "Push: O(1) — write at top, increment. Pop: O(1) — decrement, return. Peek: O(1) — read top. isEmpty/size: O(1) — the pointer is the counter. All worst-case on fixed buffers, amortized with doubling growth. Min-stack: O(1) getMin via parallel minima. Monotonic push: O(1) amortized. Space: n elements + O(1) bookkeeping; linked version adds a pointer per element."], ["depth check", "O(1)", "log len(stack) per loop iteration; climbing = leak", "Underflow with a clear conscience: the stack is empty because a push never happened — trace the push path, not the pop. In parsers, print the stack top at each closer for small inputs; the mismatch shows the exact nesting violation. Wrong output order: you used a stack where a queue belonged (or vice versa) — check BFS-vs-DFS first, it's the most common silent-order bug in the business."]]; /* [op, complexity, note, why] */
